<BaseLayout title="Machine-Native Complexity Standard — Poor Louis Labs" description="The flagship Poor Louis Labs program for evidence-controlled machine-native implementation complexity.">
<div class="shell">
<section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">Flagship research and standards program</div><h1>Machine-Native Complexity Standard</h1><p class="lede">An open, experimental, community-developed, tool-neutral engineering standard for accepting generated or machine-optimized implementations that may exceed ordinary human-maintainability limits.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/epi13/machine-native-complexity-standard">Open repository</a><a class="button" href="https://github.com/epi13/machine-native-complexity-standard/blob/main/spec/MNCS-v0.2.md">Read MNCS 0.2</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section prose"><div class="eyebrow">Core proposition</div><h2>Human readability is relocated, not eliminated.</h2><p>Readable control moves to the contract, reference behavior, limits, policy, evidence, provenance, development record, regeneration, rollback, and governance. Machines may own internal execution complexity only when it purchases a predeclared measurable benefit inside an auditable correctness, safety, resource, provenance, development, and regeneration envelope.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Experimental status.</strong> MNCS and MNCDS are not accredited ISO, ANSI, IEEE, IETF, or equivalent standards. A validator result is scoped to the declared contract, environment, evidence, policy, identities, and process record.</div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-head"><h2>Two complementary claims</h2><p>Implementation evidence and development-process control are evaluated independently.</p></div><div class="level-grid"><article class="level"><div class="kicker">MNCS L1–L5</div><h3>Implementation and evidence</h3><p>Evaluates behavioral conformance, safety, structural invariants, measurable benefit, provenance, regeneration, rollback, and audit evidence.</p></article><article class="level"><div class="kicker">MNCDS D1–D4</div><h3>Development process</h3><p>Evaluates generation controls, immutable baselines, evidence partitions, selection discipline, evaluator independence, release controls, regeneration, monitoring, and retirement.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-head"><h2>Conformance structure</h2><p>Levels and profiles are cumulative within their own families. UNKNOWN never silently counts as PASS.</p></div><div class="level-grid"><div>{[['L1','Behavioral conformance and readable control'],['L2','Runtime, memory, malformed-input, and resource safety'],['L3','Tool-neutral structural invariants'],['L4','Repeated measurement and useful-benefit threshold'],['L5','Locked regeneration, rollback, and audit']].map(([level,text]) => <article class="level"><div class="kicker">MNCS-{level}</div><strong>{text}</strong></article>)}</div><div>{[['D1','Controlled generation and candidate identity'],['D2','Pinned experimentation and reproducibility'],['D3','Protected selection and independent evaluation'],['D4','Release, rollback, regeneration, monitoring, retirement']].map(([level,text]) => <article class="level"><div class="kicker">MNCDS-{level}</div><strong>{text}</strong></article>)}</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-head"><h2>System model</h2><p>The diagram is an orientation aid; the specification remains authoritative.</p></div><div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="A readable contract and reference behavior govern candidate generation. Candidate evidence and independent analysis are evaluated by MNCS. A separate MNCDS record governs selection, release, regeneration, rollback, and retirement."><div><strong>Readable control</strong><br />Contract · reference · limits · policy</div><div><strong>Candidate and evidence</strong><br />Identity · behavior · safety · structure · performance</div><div><strong>Lifecycle control</strong><br />Selection · release · regeneration · rollback · retirement</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-head"><h2>Ecosystem</h2><p>Related repositories provide independent validation, optional analysis, and exploratory evidence without collapsing their distinct assurance boundaries.</p></div><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>MNCS reference repository</h3><p>Normative specifications, schemas, Python validators, examples, governance, RFCs, packages, trust policy, and interoperability corpus.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>MNCS Validator for Rust</h3><p>An independent offline implementation of the interoperable validation subset—not a wrapper around the Python validator.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Joern Agent Bridge</h3><p>An optional structural-analysis provider and graph-grounded agent tool. Joern is not a normative MNCS dependency.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>GraphFlow Agent Bench</h3><p>A paired benchmark producing exploratory workflow evidence. Its initial demonstration does not establish an advantage for the treatment workflow.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="section-head"><h2>Entry points</h2><p>Choose the path that matches your role.</p></div><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Understand</h3><p><a href="https://github.com/epi13/machine-native-complexity-standard/blob/main/docs/introduction.md">Read the introduction</a> and the evidence-derived conformance model.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Try</h3><p><a href="https://github.com/epi13/machine-native-complexity-standard/blob/main/docs/quickstart.md">Validate a minimal example</a> with the offline CLI.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Participate</h3><p>Review the <a href="https://github.com/epi13/machine-native-complexity-standard/blob/main/GOVERNANCE.md">governance model</a>, contribution guide, and RFC process.</p></article></div></section>
</div>
</BaseLayout>
